# tests/recursion/relative.rxt -- module `recursion` ([DD-14]): SS2.3: `(?±N)`/`\g<±N>` at four distances, forward and backward, the leading-zero and relative-zero cells
#
# GENERATED BY tests/recursion/gen_corpus.py, and that is a property rather
# than a convenience: every expectation below -- including every `g` line --
# was produced by driving the cell through libpcre2 10.46 (via the committed
# ctypes binding at docs/design/subroutines_measurements/probes/sr_oracle.py,
# which borrows la_oracle.py -> br_oracle.py -> pcre2_ctypes.py) BEFORE it was
# written. The generator never asks pcrec anything.
#
# THE SOLE ORACLE IS LIBPCRE2 -- THERE IS NO PYTHON ARM, AND THAT IS AN
# ABSENCE RATHER THAN A DIVERGENCE (design SS10.1). python3 `re` has no
# subroutine-call construct at all: every one of the nine call spellings plus
# both zero spellings raises `re.error` at compile time. `\\1` and `(?P=n)`
# DO compile in python, but they are `backrefs`' reference construct, not this
# module's call construct (design SS2.1's one-cell discriminator is why
# `spellings.rxt` opens with it) -- so marking this corpus python-verifiable
# on the strength of those two spellings would be checking the wrong module.
#
# `(?(DEFINE)...)` DOES NOT APPEAR ANYWHERE IN THIS CORPUS. It is module
# `conditionals`'s construct until D71 decision 4's registry row lands
# ([DD-14] wave F, not started when this corpus was written); every callee-
# only body below uses the oracle-verified `{0}`-callee idiom instead
# (design SS2.5/SS4.4c).
#
# NOTHING IN src/ IMPLEMENTS A SUBROUTINE CALL YET. Every ordinary `m`/`n`
# block in this corpus is therefore EXPECTED to report a pattern-compile
# failure under today's harness (`requires module 'recursion'`) -- that is
# the correct, documented state before [DD-14] wave B+C lands (docs/
# testing.md's "expected-unsupported" policy), not a corpus defect.
#
# Design: docs/design/subroutines_design.md. Decisions: docs/dev/decisions.md
# D71 (the give-up-code axis, D71.1) and D72 (PCREC_ERR_INTERNAL).

# ====================================================================
# BACKWARD `(?-N)`, at distances 1..4
# ====================================================================

# R1. nearest group to the left = group 2.
pattern ^(a)(b)(?-1)$
features recursion
m "abb" 0 3
g 1 0 1
g 2 1 2

# R2. = group 1.
pattern ^(a)(b)(?-2)$
features recursion
m "aba" 0 3
g 1 0 1
g 2 1 2

# R3. = group 1.
pattern ^(a)(b)(c)(?-3)$
features recursion
m "abca" 0 4
g 1 0 1
g 2 1 2
g 3 2 3

# R4. = group 1.
pattern ^(a)(b)(c)(d)(?-4)$
features recursion
m "abcda" 0 5
g 1 0 1
g 2 1 2
g 3 2 3
g 4 3 4

# ====================================================================
# FORWARD `(?+N)`, at distances 1..4 -- THE SHAPE THAT MAKES A CALL UNLIKE A REFERENCE (design SS2.3): the target's pattern runs BEFORE the target group does.
# ====================================================================

# F1. group 1's own pattern runs via the call first.
pattern ^(?+1)(a)$
features recursion
m "aa" 0 2
g 1 1 2

# F2. counts forward past one group to reach the second.
pattern ^(?+2)(a)(b)$
features recursion
m "bab" 0 3
g 1 1 2
g 2 2 3

# F3.
pattern ^(?+3)(a)(b)(c)$
features recursion
m "cabc" 0 4
g 1 1 2
g 2 2 3
g 3 3 4

# F4.
pattern ^(?+4)(a)(b)(c)(d)$
features recursion
m "dabcd" 0 5
g 1 1 2
g 2 2 3
g 3 3 4
g 4 4 5

# ====================================================================
# LEADING ZEROS ON A RELATIVE VALUE (design SS2.4a) -- accepted, same target as without the zero.
# ====================================================================

# EXPECTED-UNSUPPORTED, WAVE D (APPROACH.md SS7, docs/testing.md step 2). The construct is REAL and this cell is oracle-verified below; pcrec cannot compile it until wave D wires the port, so the block pins the REFUSAL and carries the answer. Wave D deletes the `wave=` argument in gen_corpus.py and re-runs it.
# WAVE D ORACLE: 'abb' at startpos 0 -> (0,3) g1=(0,1) g2=(1,2)
# MEASURED, this build: pcrec: module 'recursion' is enabled but \g is not implemented yet (pattern offset 7)
pattern ^(a)(b)\g<-01>$
features recursion
perr

pattern ^(a)(b)(?-01)$
features recursion
m "abb" 0 3
g 1 0 1
g 2 1 2

# EXPECTED-UNSUPPORTED, WAVE D (APPROACH.md SS7, docs/testing.md step 2). The construct is REAL and this cell is oracle-verified below; pcrec cannot compile it until wave D wires the port, so the block pins the REFUSAL and carries the answer. Wave D deletes the `wave=` argument in gen_corpus.py and re-runs it.
# WAVE D ORACLE: 'aba' at startpos 0 -> (0,3) g1=(0,1) g2=(1,2)
# MEASURED, this build: pcrec: module 'recursion' is enabled but \g is not implemented yet (pattern offset 7)
pattern ^(a)(b)\g<-02>$
features recursion
perr

# ====================================================================
# `\g<+-N>` OBEYS THE SAME RELATIVE RULE the `(?` doorway does.
# ====================================================================

# EXPECTED-UNSUPPORTED, WAVE D (APPROACH.md SS7, docs/testing.md step 2). The construct is REAL and this cell is oracle-verified below; pcrec cannot compile it until wave D wires the port, so the block pins the REFUSAL and carries the answer. Wave D deletes the `wave=` argument in gen_corpus.py and re-runs it.
# WAVE D ORACLE: 'aa' at startpos 0 -> (0,2) g1=(1,2)
# MEASURED, this build: pcrec: module 'recursion' is enabled but \g is not implemented yet (pattern offset 1)
pattern ^\g<+1>(a)$
features recursion
perr

# EXPECTED-UNSUPPORTED, WAVE D (APPROACH.md SS7, docs/testing.md step 2). The construct is REAL and this cell is oracle-verified below; pcrec cannot compile it until wave D wires the port, so the block pins the REFUSAL and carries the answer. Wave D deletes the `wave=` argument in gen_corpus.py and re-runs it.
# WAVE D ORACLE: 'abb' at startpos 0 -> (0,3) g1=(0,1) g2=(1,2)
# MEASURED, this build: pcrec: module 'recursion' is enabled but \g is not implemented yet (pattern offset 7)
pattern ^(a)(b)\g<-1>$
features recursion
perr

# ====================================================================
# A RELATIVE VALUE OF ZERO IS ALWAYS ERROR 126 (design SS2.4a) -- in every spelling, leading zero or not.
# ====================================================================

# `(?-00)`
# libpcre2 ALSO refuses to compile this: pcre2_compile failed at offset 8: a relative value of zero is not allowed
pattern (a)(?-00)
features recursion
perr

# `(?+00)`
# libpcre2 ALSO refuses to compile this: pcre2_compile failed at offset 8: a relative value of zero is not allowed
pattern (a)(?+00)
features recursion
perr

# `(?-0)`
# libpcre2 ALSO refuses to compile this: pcre2_compile failed at offset 7: a relative value of zero is not allowed
pattern (a)(?-0)
features recursion
perr

# `\g<-0>`
# libpcre2 ALSO refuses to compile this: pcre2_compile failed at offset 5: a relative value of zero is not allowed
pattern (a)\g<-0>
features recursion
perr
